numeric
AND
not bool
AND
finite
AND
domain-valid


---

⚠️ 2. NaN / infinity are still possible

For example:

float("nan")
float("inf")

can enter numerical paths unless explicitly rejected.

For a simulation engine, this should eventually become a hard validation boundary.

Your earlier instinct about this was correct.


---

⚠️ 3. Your run() method is not naturally reusable

ResilienceLoop.run() modifies:

self.current_tick
self.audit_log
self.system

So calling:

loop.run()
loop.run()

does not represent two fresh experiments.

The second invocation inherits the previous state.

That isn't necessarily wrong—but it needs to be explicit.

Your Monte Carlo benchmark correctly creates a fresh AdversarialSystem per run. The resilience loop itself currently doesn't.


---

⚠️ 4. tick_delay isn't part of the model dynamics

This:

time.sleep(self.tick_delay)

changes wall-clock execution but not the simulated system.

That's fine for demonstration pacing.

But conceptually:

simulation time ≠ real execution time

Keep those separate when you eventually build the formal model.


